Constipation
Geriatric & Frailty
Infrequent hard pellet-like stools, Excessive painful straining, Sensation of incomplete evacuation, Colonic faecal loading on X-ray

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-011
Reason for Visit: Acute severe lower abdominal pain, intractable straining, and suspected faecal impaction leading to near-syncope on the toilet.
Location: Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, severe lower abdominal cramping with repeated unsuccessful attempts to pass stool, resulting in a pre-syncopal episode from excessive vagal stimulation (straining). Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for faecal impaction, mechanical obstruction, or bowel perforation, providing immediate disimpaction and relief prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower stomach is in absolute agony, I have been pushing for hours but it feels like there is a rock stuck down there that won't come out."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a one-week history of infrequent bowel movements (none in the last four days). Prior to this, they noted the passage of hard, dry, and pellet-like stools requiring excessive and painful straining to successfully initiate defecation. Following a bowel movement, they consistently felt a sensation of incomplete rectal evacuation. Today, this progressed to an acute sensation of anorectal obstruction or a physical blockade. The patient admits to the necessity of using digital manual manoeuvres earlier today to facilitate stool extraction, which was unsuccessful and highly painful. This evening, they developed severe generalized lower abdominal bloating and discomfort, accompanied by poor appetite and early satiety over the last 48 hours. They currently deny nausea or recurrent vomiting.
Functional Status: Normally mobilizes with a frame; currently bedbound post-syncopal episode and guarding their abdomen.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of bowel perforation or recurrent syncope and head strike from straining.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/92 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, diaphoretic, and lying in a fetal position holding their lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals visible lower abdominal distension. Palpation demonstrates a firm, highly loaded descending colon and sigmoid colon with voluntary guarding but no rebound tenderness or rigidity. Bowel sounds are present and active. Digital rectal examination (DRE) reveals a vault completely packed with rock-hard, unyielding faeces (faecal impaction) located immediately proximal to the anal verge. (Colonic faecal loading on X-ray is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding, though clinically evident on DRE).
Environmental Observations: The bathroom call bell was utilized; patient is currently back in bed with a history of a near-fall in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient was recently started on oral codeine for osteoarthritis without concurrent initiation of a stimulant laxative.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Constipation with acute lower rectal faecal impaction - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Large Bowel Obstruction (Malignant) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of hard stool in the rectal vault on DRE, absence of profound weight loss or PR bleeding, and direct correlation with recent opiate initiation.

Acute Diverticulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of fever, localized left lower quadrant peritonism (rebound/guarding), and a clear mechanical cause identified on PR exam.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of right lower quadrant pain, normal temperature, and chronicity of symptoms directly relating to bowel habit.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute pain crisis and a pre-syncopal episode secondary to severe straining against a completely impacted rectal vault. Telephone triage correctly identified the risk of a dangerous vagal response or underlying mechanical obstruction. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in unmanaged severe pain, repeated syncopal attempts in the bathroom risking catastrophic traumatic injury, and the potential for stercoral ulceration or perforation. This urgent after-hours assessment allowed for immediate physical identification of the impaction via DRE, execution of manual disimpaction and enema administration, and immediate cessation of the offending opiate medication, successfully resolving the acute crisis and averting an emergency department transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed gentle manual fragmentation of the distal faecal bolus followed by the administration of a micro-enema (Microlax), which successfully yielded a large volume of hard stool and immediate pain relief. Ceased oral codeine immediately. Commenced regular macrogol (Movicol) two sachets daily and senna 2 tablets at night.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured hydration and post-prandial toileting routine. Step 1: The patient sits on the toilet for five minutes approximately 30 minutes after consuming breakfast, utilizing a small footstool to elevate their knees above their hips. Step 2: Upon successful completion of this daily toileting routine, the nursing staff provides positive reinforcement by delivering the patient's preferred morning newspaper or magazine. Cause-and-effect: Establishing a routine that leverages the natural gastrocolic reflex, combined with an immediate social reward, ensures consistent bowel habits and prevents the dangerous recurrence of impaction.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Instructed facility staff to implement a strict bowel chart.
DME/Safety (Optional): Prescribed a toilet footstool (squatty potty equivalent) to optimize anorectal angle for defecation.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 3-4 days to monitor bowel function and reassess osteoarthritis analgesia.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient develops sudden, severe, and unrelenting abdominal pain, a high fever, vomiting that prevents keeping fluids down, or visible red blood in the stool.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The acute blockage has been cleared, but the colon remains sluggish and loaded higher up; the new laxative regimen is critical.
Clinical Course: Bowel movements should become softer and more frequent over the next 48-72 hours. Mild cramping is expected as the laxatives begin to move the remaining stool.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Alert the nursing staff immediately if your stomach suddenly becomes rock hard and incredibly painful, if you start vomiting, or if you develop a fever. A doctor must review your regular pain medications next week.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The codeine you were taking for your joints effectively paralyzed your bowel, causing the stool to turn into a hard rock that you could not pass. We have cleared the blockage from below, and the new medications will draw water into your bowel to keep the stool soft and moving.
Actionable Steps: You must drink at least 1.5 litres of water a day for the new laxative powders to work. Sit on the toilet every morning 30 minutes after breakfast with your feet elevated on a stool, and enjoy your newspaper afterward as a reward for sticking to the routine.

SMS INFORMATION

